Here is a Java application that generates and displays all prime numbers in the range of 1 to n, where n is a specified integer.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PrimeNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 20;
        List<Integer> primes = getPrimes(n);
        System.out.println("Prime numbers in the range of 1 to " + n + ":");
        for (int prime : primes) {
            System.out.print(prime + " ");
        }
    }

    public static List<Integer> getPrimes(int n) {
        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i)) {
                primes.add(i);
            }
        }
        return primes;
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        if (num <= 3) {
            return true;
        }
        if (num % 2 == 0 || num % 3 == 0) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 5; i * i <= num; i += 6) {
            if (num % i == 0 || num % (i + 2) == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

This Java application works by first defining a method `getPrimes` which generates a list of prime numbers in the range of 1 to a given number `n`. It does this by iterating over the numbers from 2 to `n` and adding each number to the list if it is prime.

The `isPrime` method is a helper method that checks if a given number is prime. It does this by first checking if the number is less than or equal to 1, in which case it is not prime. It then checks if the number is less than or equal to 3, in which case it is prime. It then checks if the number is divisible by 2 or 3, in which case it is not prime. Finally, it checks if the number is divisible by any number up to the square root of `num`, which is an efficient way to check for primality.

In the `main` method, we call the `getPrimes` method with `n` set to 20 and print the resulting list of prime numbers.

When you run this Java application, it will output all the prime numbers in the range of 1 to 20: 

```bash
Prime numbers in the range of 1 to 20:
2 3 5 7 11 13 17 19
```

This output is correct, as it includes all prime numbers in the range of 1 to 20.

You can use java.sql to fetch the result from the MySQL database. Here is an example:

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.sql.Statement;

public class Main {
    private static final String URL = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/database";
    private static final String USER = "root";
    private static final String PASSWORD = "root";

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Connection connection = null;
        Statement statement = null;
        ResultSet resultSet = null;
        int count = 0;

        try {
            connection = DriverManager.getConnection(URL, USER, PASSWORD);
            statement = connection.createStatement();
            resultSet = statement.executeQuery("select count(*) from employeedetails");

            if (resultSet.next()) {
                count = resultSet.getInt(1);
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            try {
                if (resultSet != null) resultSet.close();
                if (statement != null) statement.close();
                if (connection != null) connection.close();
            } catch (SQLException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        System.out.println(count);
    }
}
```

You need to replace `database` with your database name, `root` with your database user, and `root` with your database password in the `URL`, `USER`, and `PASSWORD` variables. You also need to replace `employeedetails` with your table name in the query string.

This code connects to the database, executes the query, reads the result into a ResultSet, retrieves the first value from the ResultSet, and stores it in the `count` variable. It then prints `count` to the console.

Make sure to handle exceptions and close resources properly.

