To create the interactive user registration interface, you can use HTML for the structure, CSS for styling, and JavaScript for interactions. Here is a basic example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>User Registration Form</title>
    <style>
        form {
            width: 300px;
            margin: 0 auto;
        }
        input[type="submit"] {
            width: 100%;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <form>
        <label for="name">Name:</label>
        <input type="text" id="name" name="name"><br><br>
        <label for="email">Email:</label>
        <input type="email" id="email" name="email"><br><br>
        <label for="phone">Phone:</label>
        <input type="tel" id="phone" name="phone"><br><br>
        <input type="submit" value="Submit">
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

The above HTML code defines a simple registration form. You can add more fields as needed. 

For the JavaScript part, you can validate the input before submitting the form. Here’s an example:

```javascript
document.getElementById('form').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
  event.preventDefault();

  var name = document.getElementById('name').value;
  var email = document.getElementById('email').value;
  var phone = document.getElementById('phone').value;

  if (name === '' || email === '' || phone === '') {
    alert('Please fill in all fields');
  } else {
    alert('Form submitted');
  }
});
```

This JavaScript will prevent the form from submitting if the name, email, or phone fields are empty. It will also display an alert if the form is submitted.

You can add more validation logic as needed. For example, you could check if the email address is in a valid format, or if the phone number is in a valid format.

Please note, the above code does not handle form submission and data storage. For that, you may need to use a server-side language like PHP, Python, or Node.js.
